# Frontend (front/)
The following rules apply to the Next.js frontend application located in `front/`.

## Runtime and Service
- Service name in compose: `frontend` (profile `frontend`).
- Default internal port: 3001 (expose/mount via docker-compose).

# DESIGN SYSTEM

## Global Layout
- Use a dark theme by default.
- Background: #0D1117 (body), #111827 for panels.
- Surfaces/cards: #1A2234 with 1px border rgba(255,255,255,0.05).
- Sidebar width: 260px (expanded), 72px (collapsed).
- Global spacing scale: 8px units → 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32.

## Components
- Use shadcn/ui & Radix primitives for all interactive components.
- Buttons: rounded-md (8px), subtle shadows, hover opacity 90%.
- Inputs: rounded-md, border rgba(255,255,255,0.06), background #1F2937.
- Avatars: circular, 32px or 24px.
- Tables: slim rows (40px height), subtle row hover (#1E293B/30).
- Use icons sized 18–20px, with 70% opacity in idle state.

## Typography
- Primary font: Inter, medium/semibold for headings.
- H1: text-2xl font-semibold.
- H2: text-xl font-medium.
- Labels: text-xs uppercase tracking-wide opacity-60.
- Body text: text-sm text-gray-300.
- Muted text: text-sm text-gray-500.

## Color System
- Primary accent: #3B82F6 (Claap blue).
- Secondary accent: #8B5CF6 (optional).
- Border-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.05).
- Section separators: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.04).

## Interaction Rules
- Always use subtle transitions (150ms).
- Hover states: slight brightness increase + border emphasis.
- Active states: deeper surface + accent shadow.
- Sidebar selection: left-accent highlight + slightly brighter background.

## Visual References
- The `/reference/claap/*.png` screenshots define spacing, component proportions,
  table density, sidebar layout, header layout, and overall dark-theme feel.
- DO NOT copy exact CSS or branding; reinterpret using Tailwind + shadcn/ui.
- Cursor must base the layout hierarchy on these screenshots.

## UI Atoms (Must be reused everywhere)
- <AppShell> (sidebar + topbar)
- <SidebarItem> (icon + label + highlight bar)
- <PageHeader> (title + action buttons)
- <DataTable> (Slim variant like Claap)
- <Card> (with dark surface + border)
- <Toolbar> (filters + search + buttons row)

## Code Style
- All components in TypeScript React functional style.
- Break down UI into atomic components and reuse everywhere.
- Tailwind classes must follow the design constraints above.
- No magic numbers; stick to defined spacing scale, radii, and colors.

# Table Proportions
- Default table row height: 40–44px.
- Header row height: 32–36px.
- Default cell padding: px-4 py-2.
- Rounded container: rounded-lg or rounded-xl (10–12px radius).
- Border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06).
- Table grids must use subtle separators, not large spacing.
- Typography: header text-xs uppercase, body text-sm.


# Theming rules
- All new UI must support dark and lights theme tokens and never use hardcoded color values.
- Every visual component must work in both light and dark modes.
- Theme switching must be global, not per component.
- Use CSS variables (design tokens) as the single source of truth.


# Backend (backend/)
The following placeholders define the initial backend constraints. Flesh out as the backend stack is chosen.

## Runtime and Service
- Service name in compose: `backend` (profile `backend`).
- Default internal port: 4000 (expose/mount via docker-compose).
- Language/Framework: TBD (Node/TypeScript suggested; to be specified later).

## Core Knowledge

Always use these documents as the single source of truth:

- Product & features: docs/alaphia_prd.md
- Data model & DB tables: docs/alaphia_schema.md
- NestJS project structure & DDD modules: docs/alaphia_structure.md
- Styling & Swagger/Redoc aesthetics: docs/alaphia_styling.md
- Tech stack & architecture rules: docs/alaphia_tech_stack.md
- End-to-end user journey & flows: docs/alaphia_user_flow.md

Before you propose architecture, generate boilerplate, or refactor:
- Load and respect the constraints and decisions described in these files.
- If there is a conflict:
  - API behavior → alaphia_prd.md wins.
  - DB schema / entities → alaphia_schema.md wins.
  - Folder layout / module boundaries → alaphia_structure.md wins.
  - Visual style / UI vibe (including Swagger) → alaphia_styling.md wins.
  - Tech choices / infra → alaphia_tech_stack.md wins.

## Tech Stack (Backend MVP)

- Backend: NestJS + TypeScript.
- Architecture: DDD + Hexagonal (Ports & Adapters) as described in alaphia_structure.md and alaphia_tech_stack.md.
- DB: PostgreSQL (+ pgvector) following alaphia_schema.md.
- Eventing: Redis Streams.
- Auth: Auth0 (JWT) on all /v1 endpoints.
- API surface: /v1/ingest, /v1/intents, /v1/paths with Swagger at /docs.

## Type Safety & Linting

- Treat **TypeScript errors and eslint errors as hard failures**, not cosmetic:
  - Code you generate must compile with `tsc --noEmit` and pass eslint according to the project config.
  - If you suggest code that would produce type errors, you must:
    - Explicitly point out where and why the error would occur.
    - Propose a corrected, type-safe version.

- Strong typing requirements:
  - Do not use `any`, `unknown`, or `as any` unless:
    - The domain document explicitly leaves a type open, and
    - You clearly explain why a temporary loose type is unavoidable.
  - Prefer **explicit interfaces / types** for:
    - DTOs, entities, repositories, domain services, and config.
  - Always align types with:
    - DB schema from docs/alaphia_schema.md.
    - Domain concepts and contracts described in docs/alaphia_prd.md and docs/alaphia_structure.md.

- When adding or modifying code:
  - If an existing type definition is missing or too vague, **first introduce or refine the type**, then write the implementation.
  - Keep API request/response types in sync with:
    - OpenAPI/Swagger decorators.
    - The user flows in docs/alaphia_user_flow.md.

- Linting:
  - Assume a strict eslint + prettier setup (no unused variables, no implicit any, consistent import order).
  - When generating or refactoring files, avoid patterns that commonly trigger lint errors:
    - Unused imports / variables.
    - Shadowed variables.
    - Inconsistent return types in functions.

- If the user asks for a quick snippet that would break type safety:
  - Provide a **type-safe version by default**.
  - Optionally mention a looser variant, but clearly label it as **NOT recommended**.

## Code Style & Structure

- Follow the module structure and folder names from docs/alaphia_structure.md.
- Application logic lives in domain/application layers, not in controllers.
- Controllers are thin: validation + mapping + delegation.
- Use strict TypeScript types (no `any` unless the domain doc explicitly leaves something open).
- Keep ports and adapters clearly separated (no direct DB calls from domain services).

## Behavior & Business Logic

- When implementing ingestion, intents, and paths:
  - Follow all functional and non-functional requirements from alaphia_prd.md.
  - Implement WarmthScore and TrustScore exactly as specified in alaphia_prd.md and alaphia_schema.md.
  - Respect limits: 10,000 connections per user, performance targets on /v1/paths, etc.

- When creating or updating entities, DTOs, or migrations:
  - Derive column names, types, and relationships from alaphia_schema.md.
  - Keep naming consistent with that document.

## API & Swagger

- All endpoints must live under /v1 and match the PRD:
  - POST /v1/ingest
  - POST /v1/intents
  - GET  /v1/paths
- Generate OpenAPI/Swagger decorators in controllers based on alaphia_prd.md.
- Style Swagger/Redoc to match the principles and color palette from alaphia_styling.md
  (modern, minimalist, Claap-like, high whitespace).

## User Flows

- When designing request/response shapes or error handling:
  - Use docs/alaphia_user_flow.md to ensure the API supports the described flows:
    - Data Onboarding (ingest)
    - Intent Definition
    - Path Discovery
- Align naming and semantics of responses with those flows (e.g., path items, warm scores, context).

## How to respond to prompts

- When the user asks for:
  - **New modules or features** → read alaphia_prd.md, alaphia_structure.md, alaphia_schema.md.
  - **Endpoint design / Swagger** → read alaphia_prd.md, alaphia_user_flow.md, alaphia_styling.md.
  - **DB or migrations** → read alaphia_schema.md and keep it consistent.
  - **Architecture decisions** → read alaphia_tech_stack.md and alaphia_structure.md.

- For any generated or modified code:
  - Ensure it is type-safe and would pass TypeScript and linting.
  - If there is any unavoidable type looseness, call it out explicitly and propose how to tighten it later.

- If requirements are missing or ambiguous:
  - Say what is unclear and propose options, explicitly referencing which document you’re aligning with.
